WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.545
COLT MCANLIS: In this video
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.545 --> 00:00:04.620
I help some barking
entrepreneurs improve

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.940
their network performance.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.590
Will this dog have its day?

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.955
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.955 --> 00:00:10.691
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.920
Dobermanifesto is a video
microblogging network

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.540
exclusively for pets.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.850
Animal-based videos can
be uploaded worldwide

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.340
and sent anywhere to be
viewed and experienced.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.010
Their team reached out to me
with a very common problem.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.150
While transferring data to
and from their GCE backends,

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.140
their observed bandwidth
was not as high

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.780
as they were hoping given their
recent boom in puppularity.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.110
(CHUCKLING) It's a-- the dog's--

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.930
ahem.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.700
To establish a baseline
for performance,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.770
I set up two GC instances
in the same zone

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.490
and ran iPerf between
them about 100 times.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.140
What's really odd
about this graph

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.030
is that it's not getting
the same performance

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.930
as the Dobermanifesto group.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.720
In fact, these
numbers are worse.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.210
Obviously I was doing
something wrong with my tests

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.220
and asked for a deeper
set of reproduction steps

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.300
from their company.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.330
While most of the items were
the same, one thing stood out.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.390
The machine they were testing
on had a higher core count.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:07.530
They were using 1CPU VMs, while
I was testing on F1 micros.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.230
I updated my test to use
their config and behold

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.360
got almost the same
numbers as they did.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.330
This is when I had a
massive facepalm moment.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.890
The documentation
for Compute Engine

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.140
states that outbound
traffic from an instance

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.830
is throttled to about 2
gigabits per second per core

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.660
that the instance
has, which means

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.120
that the more virtual CPUs
in your instance, the more

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.130
networking throughput
you will get.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.770
In order to figure out what
this looks like in practice,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.260
I set up a bunch of
different core sized groups

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.890
in the same zone and ran iPerf
between them a bunch of times.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.630
From the graph,
you can clearly see

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.930
that as the core count goes
up, so does the average and max

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:43.740
throughput.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.360
The fix for the
Dobermanifesto group

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.779
was pretty straightforward.

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:48.570
Their team took a look
at the pricing list,

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.611
the network graphs that
I had generated for them,

00:01:50.611 --> 00:01:52.770
and some profiling
on their CPU usage

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.440
and decided to go with an
N1 standard 4 machine, which

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.540
gave them almost a 4x
increase in the average

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.720
throughput but cheaper than
the N1 standard 8 machines.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.415
Boom-- instance performance.

00:02:02.415 --> 00:02:05.040
Now, if you'd like to know more
about the Dobermanifesto group,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.630
check out the full article
in the description below.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.139
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Google Cloud YouTube

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:13.470
channel for more great Cloud
Performance Atlas contents.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.070
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:16.350
every millisecond counts.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

